for a president accused of inaction  this was a decisive moment today i  authorized two operations in iraq  targeted airstrikes to protect our  american personnel  and a humanitarian effort to help save  thousands of iraqi civilians  who are trapped on a mountain without  food and water  and facing almost certain death america  is coming to help these people he said  desperate iraqi christians yazidis and  shia  forced from their homes seeking shelter  in the mountains  the unforgiving terrain has nothing on  the threat they run from  the group formerly known as isis now the  islamic state  whose message rings far and wide convert  or be killed the u.s says the situation  threatens to become a humanitarian  catastrophe  which is why president obama ordered an  aid drop on mount sinjab  in the early hours of this morning in  recent days  yazidi women men and children from the  area of sinjar have fled for their lives  and thousands perhaps tens of thousands  are now hiding high up on the mountain  with little but the clothes on their  backs they're without food  they're without water people are  starving  and children are dying of thirst earlier  in the day the president met his  national security team to discuss the  situation which is undoubtedly  urgent  outside the white house there were  desperate calls for action  islamic state fighters continue to  spread mayhem and fear  taking 14 towns in 24 hours  they raised their black flag at the  border of the kurdish semi-autonomous  region  as they continue their aggressive surge  towards erbil  the u.s may have authorized air strikes  but that shouldn't be read  as war the president was very keen to  point out that those strikes were to  protect  u.s personnel in beale the obama  administration  mantra is very clear there is no u.s  military solution to the problems in  iraq but  if this is the start of a genocide they  may be forced to stick to their other  rule  that the world cannot sit by and watch  it happen  in irbil nearly 300 christian refugees  sought sanctuary in a cathedral  their persecution does bear the terrible  hallmarks of ethnic cleansing  and their plight could lead the u.s to  act further  but the long-term hope is still that the  divided iraqi government  can save its own country this is after  all a u.s government  intent on ending wars not starting them  amanda walker sky news washington
 

